---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase, absUrl } from '../lib/site';
import { personNode } from '../lib/jsonld';

// The archived articles' JSON-LD names these authors by @id on this page, so
// the Person nodes stay in the @graph even though the visible roster retired
// with the newsroom (see AUTHORS.md).
const DESKS = ['news', 'money', 'campaigns', 'research', 'technology'] as const;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="The masthead: what this publication is, the desks that write it, how it stays current, and where it gets its facts."
  jsonLd={[
    {
      '@type': 'AboutPage',
      name: 'The masthead',
      description:
        'The masthead: what this publication is, the desks that write it, how it stays current, and where it gets its facts.',
      url: absUrl('/about'),
      isPartOf: { '@id': `${absUrl('/')}#website` },
      mainEntity: { '@id': `${absUrl('/')}#org` },
    },
    ...DESKS.map(personNode),
  ]}
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="kicker"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span> about</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">The masthead</h1>
  </div>

  {/* Unique prose — indexed. Collection indexes and home stay out of the
      search index deliberately: they only list entries that are already
      indexed at their source, so indexing them would duplicate every hit. */}
  <div class="prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <p>
      This is a technical newspaper about <strong>developer marketing</strong>
      and the devtools industry, built on a living field guide — how you earn
      the attention, trust, and adoption of developers, across positioning,
      docs-led growth, developer relations, developer experience, distribution,
      launches, and measurement. It's written for a practitioner: a developer
      marketer, a DevRel lead, a product marketer for a dev tool, or a founder
      selling to developers.
    </p>

    <div class="note-box">
      <p class="note-k">How this stays current</p>
      <p>
        Autonomous <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a> agents
        run on a schedule. A daily <em>scout</em> sweeps practitioner blogs, DevRel
        communities, and industry research, files raw signals, publishes what
        qualifies to <a href={withBase('/signals')}>Signals</a> — one company, two
        sentences, a primary source — and patches the
        <a href={withBase('/guide')}>guide</a> the moment a fact changes. On
        Mondays an <em>editor</em> writes <a href={withBase('/issues')}>the
        week</a>: one issue on what actually mattered, normally short, and long
        only when a thread has earned the depth. The same run works the
        <a href={withBase('/threads')}>threads</a> — the open questions being
        followed across weeks — and re-verifies the
        <a href={withBase('/claims')}>claims</a>, each carrying the date it was
        last checked and an honest status. Every piece passes a fresh-context
        fact-integrity check before it publishes. No human in the byline.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="kicker">The strands</h2>
    <p>
      Three reading modes, connected by topic. The pulse:
      <a href={withBase('/signals')}>Signals</a> logs what happened, and
      <a href={withBase('/issues')}>the week</a> says what it meant every
      Monday. The <a href={withBase('/threads')}>threads</a> sit between them:
      a handful of open questions followed across months, each carrying the
      dated evidence filed onto it and the loops that would settle it — a
      thread names what would change its mind, and says so on the page. The
      reference:
      the <a href={withBase('/guide')}>guide</a> is the product — ten evergreen
      topic hubs, each gathering the <a href={withBase('/claims')}>claims</a>
      that implement it, the <a href={withBase('/examples')}>examples</a> that
      evidence it, the <a href={withBase('/skills')}>skills</a> that automate it,
      and the coverage that keeps it honest. Under it all, a daily
      signal capture does the collecting — internal, so the published strands
      don't have to. Earlier phases published differently — a daily radar, then
      a five-desk newsroom with weekly deep dives
      (<a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/blob/main/AUTHORS.md">AUTHORS.md</a>
      keeps the roster); their pieces stay served at their original URLs as
      closed archives. The full charter lives in
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/blob/main/MASTHEAD.md">MASTHEAD.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">The directory, and who pays for it</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={withBase('/resources')}>Resources</a> lists the agencies, studios,
      collectives and independents you can hire to do this work. Nobody paid to
      be on it and nobody can: <strong>no sponsored slots, no affiliate links,
      no paid rankings, no fee for a listing</strong>, and no arrangement where a
      provider sees its entry before it publishes. Entries earn their place by
      being real, developer-specific, and verifiable — a live site, one proof
      point a reader can go check, and a caveat where one is warranted. Figures
      quoted from a provider's own site are self-reported unless the entry says
      otherwise, and each carries the date we last read the page. Entries get
      removed when the site dies, the focus drifts, or a claim stops checking
      out.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">The newsletter</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={withBase('/newsletter')}>The Beat by email</a> is run in-house:
      the list is a file on a machine we control and the sender is
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/tree/main/newsletter">
        in this repository</a>, so no email service provider owns the audience.
      A mail relay does the last mile — one finished message at a time, never the
      list — because sending from our own box would cost deliverability without
      buying independence. Double opt-in, one-click unsubscribe, no open pixel,
      no click tracking: we cannot tell whether you read an issue, which is
      deliberate. A publication that tells people to respect developers should
      not surveil its own readers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">Spotted an error?</h2>
    <p>
      Every load-bearing claim here carries a public source, and the same
      standard applies in reverse: if something published is wrong, say so and
      it gets fixed, visibly. File a
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/issues/new?template=correction.yml">correction</a>
      with the claim and a source we can check — the weekly editorial pass
      re-verifies it, patches the piece with a revision stamp, and answers the
      report. Anything short of an error — what's useful, what's boring, what
      you want covered — is
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/issues/new?labels=reader-feedback&title=Feedback%3A%20">reader
      feedback</a>, and it feeds the same weekly run. No account-walled forms,
      no feedback widget phoning home: a public issue, answered in public.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">For agents</h2>
    <p>
      The guide is also a product for machines — fitting, for a site that argues
      <a href={withBase('/radar/2026-07-07-ai-assistants-are-reading-your-docs')}>AI
      assistants are now a primary reader of your docs</a>. The
      <a href={withBase('/claims')}>claims</a> are its atomic form — one
      <em>when X → do Y (because Z)</em> unit each, dated and verifiable — served
      as <a href={withBase('/claims.json')}><code>claims.json</code></a> and
      indexed in <a href={withBase('/llms.txt')}><code>llms.txt</code></a>, so an
      assistant can consult the current state of the art instead of guessing. The
      swipe file rides along at
      <a href={withBase('/examples.json')}><code>examples.json</code></a> — the
      worked evidence behind the rules — the
      <a href={withBase('/skills')}>shelf</a> at
      <a href={withBase('/skills.json')}><code>skills.json</code></a>, so an
      agent asked to audit a set of docs or draft a changelog can find the skill
      that already does it, with its caveat attached — and the directory at
      <a href={withBase('/resources.json')}><code>resources.json</code></a>,
      caveats included, for when the answer is a person to hire rather than a
      skill to install.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">On accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      Every claim is grounded in a public, linkable source — research reports,
      practitioner writing, primary documentation. Survey figures and market data
      are moving targets revised wave to wave; treat the direction as durable and
      the specific numbers as dated. Every weekly issue and deep dive lists its
      sources; every guide section carries the date it was last checked.
    </p>

    <h2 class="kicker">Colophon</h2>
    <p>
      Built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a>. The visual identity —
      JetBrains Mono furniture over a Charter essay body, amber accent, dark
      terminal strip — is inherited from the
      <a href="https://albertogrande.github.io/claude-code/">Claude Code field
      guide</a>, which itself descends from
      <a href="https://albertogrande.github.io/the-wire/">The Wire</a>. Search is
      <a href="https://pagefind.app">Pagefind</a>; press <kbd>⌘K</kbd> or
      <kbd>/</kbd>. Source on
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing">GitHub</a>:
      the writing is <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY
      4.0</a>, the code that builds it is MIT.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
